{
  "original_id": "newwin-00001-p16",
  "archive_id": "BW2-N01-P16",
  "title": "A small-component bound for odd primitive non-deficient numbers with five distinct prime factors",
  "batch_label": "新WIN-1批次",
  "kind": "resolution-paper",
  "original_status": "proved",
  "scope": "For every five distinct odd primes 3 <= p_1 < p_2 < p_3 < p_4 < p_5 and every five positive integers a_1,...,a_5, let n=product_{i=1}^5 p_i^{a_i} and R=product_{i=1}^5 p_i. If sigma(n)>=2n and sigma(d)<2d for every positive proper divisor d of n, then there exists i in {1,2,3,4,5} such that p_i^{a_i+1}<2R. This includes sigma(n)=2n and has no bound on primes or exponents. The claim is exactly the frozen original five-prime assertion; no assertion for six or more primes or for the stronger R bound is submitted.",
  "gate_basis": "Current released terminal, manifest, PDF, TeX freeze, snapshot files, provenance and four audits agree.",
